href="/3-lesser-known-pandas-functions-to-be-used-with-groupby">3 Lesser-Known Pandas Functions to Be Used with Groupby</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-books-to-help-you-master-the-pandas-library">5 Books to Help You Master the Pandas Library</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/identify-patterns-in-time-series-data-with-overlapping-window-techniques-using-pandas">Identify Patterns in Time-Series Data with Overlapping Window Techniques Using Pandas</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-ways-to-apply-if-else-conditional-statements-in-pandas">5 Ways to Apply If-Else Conditional Statements in Pandas</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading11"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse11" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse11" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading11" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse11"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/python-pandas-interview-questions">Pandas Interview</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="pandas-dataframe-rename">next →</a> <a class="next" href="pandas-pivot-table">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Pandas DataFrame.query()</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>17 Mar 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 10 min read</span></div><p>Python is an amazing programming language for performing data analytics, primarily due to the wonderful ecosystem of data-centric packages it offers. Pandas, one of these packages, allows users to import and analyze data with much efficiency and effectiveness. Data analysis requires many filtering operations. Pandas DataFrame offers multiple methods to filter data in a data frame, and the query() method is one of them.</p><p>In the following tutorial, we will learn about the DataFrame.query() method using examples. But before we get started, let's discuss the basics of Python's Pandas package.</p><h2 class="h2">A Brief Introduction to Pandas</h2><p>Pandas is an open-source yet powerful Python package used for data analysis and manipulation. The Pandas package offers two primary data structures - DataFrame and Series. These data structures streamline the tabular data management processes for both textual data and numerical data. These processes include:</p><ol class="points"><li>Data Loading</li><li>Data Tabulation</li><li>Data Cleaning</li><li>Data Filtration</li><li>Handling Missing Data</li><li>Data Normalization</li><li>Data Inspection</li><li>Statistical Data Analysis</li><li>Data Saving, and a lot more.</li></ol><h2 class="h2">Understanding the Working of Pandas</h2><p>The Pandas package revolves around the idea of the DataFrame and Series objects. DataFrame objects are two-dimensional tables used for storing data in rows and columns, whereas Series objects are one-dimensional arrays used to store data only of a single type.</p><p>Pandas works with the NumPy library, which allows users to efficiently perform various numerical operations on large arrays. This integration with NumPy allows uniform and faster operations between the two packages, one tabular and one numerical.</p><p>We can create the objects of DataFrame and Series from different data sources, like CSV files, Excel files, SQL Databases, or even Python dictionaries and lists.</p><p>After installing the Pandas package, we can access multiple functions to read and write data from various sources, streamlining the process of data tabulation in any format.</p><h2 class="h2">Key Features of Pandas</h2><p>Pandas is a Python library extensively used for manipulating, cleaning, and analysing the data. The following are some of the key features and functions of Pandas:</p><p><strong>1. Data Cleaning</strong></p><p>The Pandas package provides numerous functions to clean and transform the data, such as filling in missing values, dropping rows or columns, deleting NULL values and renaming columns.</p><p><strong>2. Data Filtering and Selection</strong></p><p>The Pandas package offers a wide range of functions for data filtration and selection based on highly granular conditions. So, irrespective of the complexity of the data, we can extract the exact required information using Pandas.</p><p><strong>3. Data Aggregation</strong></p><p>With the help of Pandas, we can perform aggregation operations like pivot, groupby, and merge to summarize and restructure the data.</p><p><strong>4. Data Visualization</strong></p><p>The Pandas package integrates with the popular library Matplotlib for data visualization, allowing users to create different types of plots and charts from the data.</p><h2 class="h2">Some Advantages of using Pandas Package</h2><p>There are numerous reasons to use the Pandas Package for data analysis and manipulation, including but not limited to:</p><ol class="points"><li><strong>Efficient Data Handling:</strong> Pandas offers a functional framework for handling large datasets efficiently and effectively. The package is built on top of NumPy, ensuring faster and more efficient numerical operations.</li><li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Pandas provides an arsenal of functions and methods for data manipulation, making it a flexible tool for various data scientist and analyst activities.</li><li><strong>Easy Integration with Other Libraries:</strong> Pandas can be integrated easily and efficiently with other Python libraries, such as NumPy, Matplotlib, and SciPy, creating robust pipelines for data analytics.</li><li><strong>Readability:</strong> Pandas provide clear and concise syntax, making the code easy to read and understand. This feature ensures easy appending and maintaining of the code, driving smooth collaboration with other team members and improving the longevity of the projects.</li><li><strong>Wide Adoption and Support:</strong> Pandas is a Python package widely used in the data science community. Therefore, online forums offer plenty of resources, tutorials, and support.</li><li><strong>Handling Diverse Data Sources:</strong> Pandas can efficiently process data from diverse sources. This data processing stage includes reading and writing data sources such as Excel files, CSV files, and SQL databases. Pandas is a versatile package and a popular solution for handling data that comes in diverse sets and formats.</li></ol><h2 class="h2">Getting Started with Pandas</h2><p>Let us now understand how to start working with the Pandas library in Python.</p><h3 class="h3">Installing Pandas and Verifying the Installation</h3><p>The primary step in order to work with Pandas is to ensure whether it is installed in the system. We can install Pandas using the PIP installer by typing the following command in a command prompt or terminal.</p><p><strong>Syntax:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code"># installing the Pandas library
$ pip install pandas
</textarea></div><p>Once the installation is complete, we can verify whether the Pandas library is installed properly or not by creating a new python program file and importing the pandas module.</p><p>The following is the snippet of code illustrating the same.</p><p><strong>File: verify.py</strong></p><p>import pandas as pd</p><h4 class="n">Note: Here, pd is referred to as an alias for the Pandas. However, it is unnecessary to import the library using the alias, it just helps in writing less code every time a method or property is called.</h4><p>Now, let us save the file and run the following command in a command prompt or terminal.</p><p><strong>Syntax:</strong></p><p>$ python verify.py</p><p>The library has been installed successfully if the program does not return any importing error. In case any exception is raised, try reinstalling the library and consider checking their official documentation.</p><h3 class="h3">First Python Program using Pandas</h3><p>Now that the Pandas package is installed properly on the system, let us write our first program in Python to create a DataFrame using Pandas</p><p><strong>Example 1: Creating a DataFrame using Pandas</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code">
# importing the required module
import pandas as pd
 
# calling the DataFrame constructor
dFrame = pd.DataFrame()
print("DataFrame:", dFrame)

# initializing a list of strings
my_list = ['I', 'love', 'learning', 'Pandas', 'from', 'JavaTpoint']

# calling the DataFrame constructor on the initialized list
dFrame = pd.DataFrame(my_list)
print("DataFrame:", dFrame)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>
DataFrame: Empty DataFrame
Columns: []
Index: []
DataFrame:             0
0           I
1        love
2    learning
3      Pandas
4        from
5  JavaTpoint
</pre></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>In the above snippet of code, we have imported the pandas module as pd. We have then called the constructor of the DataFrame class. After that we have initialized a list of strings. At last, we have called the constructor of the DataFrame class on the initialized list. As a list, the first DataFrame is empty. However, the second DataFrame consists of multiple rows and columns consisting of the data from the list.</p><p>To learn more about Pandas in depth, you can visit <a href="python-pandas">the Pandas tutorial</a> available at JavaTpoint.</p><p>Now that we have the basic knowledge of Pandas, it is time for us to understand about the DataFrame.query() method in depth.</p><h2 class="h2">Understanding the Pandas DataFrame.query() Method</h2><p>The pandas.DataFrame class offers a method called query(). This method is used to query rows on the basis of the given expression (single or multiple column conditions) and returns a new DataFrame. We can also modify the existing DataFrame in place by setting the inplace argument of the query() method to True. This enables the users to efficiently filter and manipulate the data of the DataFrame without creating the additional copies.</p><p>The following are some of the key points about the DataFrame.query() method:</p><ol class="points"><li>The query() method filters the rows from a DataFrame as per the specified condition.</li><li>The query() method provides a powerful and concise syntax to filter DataFrame rows, resembling SQL queries, enhancing the readability and maintenance of code.</li><li>The query() also offers the support for a wide range of logical as well as comparison operators such as and, or, not, ==, !=, &gt;, &lt;, &gt;= and &lt;=.</li></ol><h3 class="h3">Syntax of the DataFrame.query() Method</h3><p>The syntax of the DataFrame.query() method is as follows:</p><p><strong>Syntax:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code">
DataFrame.query(expr, inplace = False, **kwargs)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Argument:</strong></p><ol class="points"><li><strong>expr:</strong> The expr argument specifies the query expression string, which follows the Python's syntax for conditional expressions.</li><li><strong>inplace:</strong> The inplace argument is a Boolean argument used to update the existing DataFrame. The default value of this argument is False.</li><li><strong>**kwargs:</strong> The **kwargs argument allows passing additional keyword arguments to the query expression. It is an optional argument.</li></ol><p><strong>Return Type:</strong></p><p>This method is used to return the filtered DataFrame.</p><p>Let us now look at the implementation of the DataFrame.query() method.</p><h3 class="h3">Implementation of the Pandas DataFrame.query() Method</h3><p>In the following section, we will look at different examples illustrating the use of the query() method to filter data in the given DataFrame.</p><p><strong>Example 1: Single Condition Filtering</strong></p><p>In this example, the data is filtered according to a single condition. Before applying the query() method, we will replace the spaces in column names with '_'.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code">
# importing the pandas module
import pandas as pd 

# creating a DataFrame using the CSV file
dataFrame = pd.read_csv("employees.csv") 
print("Unfiltered DataFrame") 
print(dataFrame)

# replacing blank spaces with '_' 
dataFrame.columns = [column.replace(" ", "_") for column in dataFrame.columns] 

# filtering the data using the DataFrame.query() method 
dataFrame.query('Senior_Management == True', inplace = True) 

print("Filtered DataFrame") 
print(dataFrame)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>
