</pre></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>In the above snippet of code, we have imported the pandas module. We have then created a DataFrame using the CSV file and printed the DataFrame for reference. We have then replaced the blank spaces in the data with the underscore, '_' and then used the filter() method to filter the DataFrame. At last, we have printed the filtered DataFrame for reference.</p><p>As a result, the data after filtering only have rows where Senior Management is set to True.</p><p><strong>Example 2: Multiple conditions filtering</strong></p><p>In this example, Dataframe has been filtered on multiple conditions. Before applying the query() method, we will replace the spaces in column names with '_'.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code">
# importing the pandas module
import pandas as pd 

# creating a DataFrame using the CSV file
dataFrame = pd.read_csv("employees.csv") 
print("Unfiltered DataFrame") 
print(dataFrame)

# replacing blank spaces with '_' 
dataFrame.columns = [column.replace(" ", "_") for column in dataFrame.columns] 

# filtering the data using the DataFrame.query() method 
dataFrame.query('Senior_Management == True and Gender == "Female" and Team == "Marketing"', inplace = True) 

